  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Sep 5–8 kinetic cycle — CENTCOM destroying eight Iranian tankers across two retaliatory operations after IRGC ballistic-missile attacks on US warships — compressed Hormuz daily transits to a 7-day rolling average of approximately 6–9 vessels, roughly 5–7% of pre-war normal. CENTCOM broke a six-day communications blackout on Sep 16, confirming three events: destruction of additional Iranian vessels after an IRGC attack targeting a US aircraft carrier with ballistic missiles, destruction of two IRGC fast boats after a USV seizure attempt, and a bilateral halt in air operations for 12 hours. No insurer has reinstated P&amp;I war-risk cover for Hormuz passage. The corridor plan Araghchi described (Sep 13, Oman) exists on paper but carries an explicit condition: the Islamabad MoU must be restored before Hormuz reopens. Under current kinetic tempo, the 30-vessel 7-day average target has no available pathway within the Oct 3 horizon. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Trump's Sep 16 statement that Iran had contacted the US &lsquo;directly&rsquo; — and the Sep 17 US visa approval for Pezeshkian and Araghchi — placed both parties in New York for the same 10-day window for the first time since the war began in February. The logistical barrier to direct contact that existed through Sep 17 no longer applies. Whether any exchange occurs, and whether it receives public confirmation from named officials on both sides, are the operative questions for the new <span class="cite">F8</span> forecast. A mutual public confirmation would be structurally distinct from the message-relay contacts that failed to meet the resolution threshold in resolved <span class="cite">F3</span>. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Araghchi's Sep 13 Muscat statement that the Iran-Oman corridor plan &lsquo;does not mean the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz&rsquo; and requires US Islamabad MoU compliance is the clearest public articulation of Iran&rsquo;s sequencing logic: political settlement first, then maritime access, not the reverse. Parliament speaker Ghalibaf&rsquo;s statement that &lsquo;the era of proportionate responses has ended&rsquo; — carried prominently in IRGC-affiliated outlets — presents the competing internal position. The two tracks — Araghchi&rsquo;s conditional diplomacy and Ghalibaf&rsquo;s escalatory framing — have coexisted throughout September, with no evidence one has displaced the other inside Iranian decision-making. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Sep 17 Israeli polling consolidation — Yashar 22 seats / Likud 18 as the modal result across four of five surveys — presents a different picture than the 21-23 seat alternation logged Sep 3. The Netanyahu bloc holds a narrow overall advantage (54 vs 52 seats projected), but the i24NEWS poll (Likud 27, Yashar 22) is the only current survey with Likud ahead of Yashar by a margin exceeding statistical noise. The bloc arithmetic still places Netanyahu in a government-formation position if the bloc total holds, complicating tactical voting incentives over the 39 remaining days. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>F7 closes today as RESOLVED-NO. The Knesset&rsquo;s natural dissolution on July 17 — the first full-term Knesset completion since 1988 — and the coalition&rsquo;s maintenance of 61/120 seats through that mandatory endpoint closed both resolution prongs. The October 27 election date was never moved earlier than the statutory date. Israeli domestic dynamics are now tracked exclusively under <span class="cite">F5</span> and the new election-related forecasts added today. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

