      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Continued IRGC ballistic-missile attacks on US warships and US tanker destructions persist beyond the UNGA window, with Ghalibaf&rsquo;s &ldquo;era of proportionate responses has ended&rdquo; framing prevailing over Araghchi&rsquo;s conditional diplomacy track.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> UNGA produces a public conditional offer (F9 fires) but no operationalized agreement, Hormuz remains at 5–10% of pre-war traffic through Q4, and the conflict freezes at current kinetic tempo with periodic flare-ups.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Pezeshkian&rsquo;s UNGA address triggers a US-accepted MoU restoration offer, N1 and N3 both resolve YES, and Iran-Oman corridor plan publication enables partial insurer re-entry into Hormuz shipping lanes before Oct 3.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; 200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Tasnim, Mehr News) framed the Sep 5–8 CENTCOM tanker destructions as systematic targeting of civilian commercial assets in violation of international maritime law. Mehr News ran the phrasing &ldquo;annihilation of Iran&rsquo;s civilian oil fleet&rdquo; across multiple dispatches; IRGC-affiliated Tasnim foregrounded &ldquo;economic warfare&rdquo; against Iranian infrastructure rather than the military designation of the targeted vessels. The Hormuz traffic collapse received no direct domestic coverage; instead, reporting emphasized Gulf partner coordination and the Sep 13 Muscat meeting as demonstrations of Iranian regional diplomatic leadership. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>The US visa approval for Pezeshkian and Araghchi received factual coverage in IRNA with emphasis on Iran&rsquo;s sovereign right to address the UN General Assembly — framed as a legal obligation on the US host country rather than any diplomatic gesture. No state-media outlet characterized the visa grant as a thaw, and no outlet used language of &ldquo;direct contact&rdquo; or &ldquo;bilateral engagement.&rdquo; → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Pezeshkian&rsquo;s Sep 1 SCO conditional offer — &ldquo;if the US returns to its commitments in the Islamabad MoU, Iran will immediately reciprocate&rdquo; — received maximum domestic play as evidence of Iranian reasonableness against US intransigence. The Islamabad MoU is consistently described across state media as a binding international agreement that the US &ldquo;unilaterally abandoned,&rdquo; positioning US compliance as a precondition rather than a negotiating point. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Araghchi&rsquo;s Sep 13 Muscat messaging ran across IRNA and Mehr as demonstrations of Iranian diplomatic initiative, with no coverage framing these as concessions or evidence of weakness. Ghalibaf&rsquo;s &ldquo;era of proportionate responses&rdquo; warning ran prominently in IRGC-affiliated Tasnim, providing a harder-line complement to the Foreign Ministry&rsquo;s conditional-diplomacy register. The two framings have appeared in parallel for the full two weeks covered here, with state media carrying both without editorial adjudication between them. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

